# Sets, Data and maps

Three kinds of value use braces or hold keys and values, and they are easy to
mix up. They are different types with different uses:

| Type | What it is | Written |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Set<T>` | distinct values of one type, with no positions | `{1, 2, 3}` |
| `Data` | a tree of named fields of any types, the shape of JSON | `{title: "Dune", pages: 412}` |
| `Map<K, V>` | keys of one type, each to a value of one type | `Map(["dune"], [412])` |

## The colon tells braces apart

Braces holding values are a set. Braces holding `key: value` entries are
`Data`. The colon is the only difference, and the empty forms keep it: `{}`
is the empty set and `{:}` is the empty `Data` record.

```d2
direction: down

braces: "a literal in braces" {shape: diamond}
colon: "has a colon?" {shape: diamond}
empty: "{:} with a declared map type?" {shape: diamond}

set: "Set<T>\n{3, 1, 2}  {}" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
data: "Data\n{title: \"Dune\"}  {:}" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
map: "Map<K, V>, OrderedMap, SortedMap\nm : Map<String, Int> = {:}" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}

braces -> colon
colon -> set: "no"
colon -> empty: "yes"
empty -> data: "no"
empty -> map: "yes"
```

```hql
{3, 1, 3, 2}
```

```text
{3, 1, 2}
```

```hql
{title: "Dune", pages: 412}
```

```text
{pages: 412, title: "Dune"}
```

```hql
{}
```

```text
{}
```

```hql
{:}
```

```text
{:}
```

A set keeps each value once. A `Data` record prints its keys in order, so the
same tree always prints the same way.

`{}` has a definite meaning, the empty set, so it is not an empty record:

```hql
d : Data = {}
d
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `d` is declared Data but its value is Set<Never>
 --> <expression>:1:12
  |
1 | d : Data = {}
  |            ^^
```

`{}` is a `Set<Never>`: a set with no elements, and so no element type yet. A
declared set type gives it one:

```hql
seen : Set<Int> = {}
seen | size
```

```text
0
```

## Data is not a map

`Data` is the type of values whose shape is not known to the program: what a
JSON or YAML document decodes to. A record's fields may hold different types,
a number beside a text beside a list, and nesting goes as deep as the
document does. It is not a hash map, and not a `Map<String, Any>`.

A `Map<K, V>` is typed: every key is a `K` and every value a `V`. The
constructor pairs a list of keys with a list of values:

```hql
Map(["dune", "emma"], [412, 474])
```

```text
Map {"dune": 412, "emma": 474}
```

Use a set for a group of distinct values, a map to look up one value per
key, and `Data` for a document whose shape the program does not control.
[[03-collections/maps|Maps]] covers maps in full.

## `{:}` is Data, or a declared map

`{:}` has no type of its own. Where nothing says otherwise it is the empty
`Data`; where a map type is declared for it, it is that empty map:

| Written | Type | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{:}` with nothing around it | `Data` | the empty `Data` record, printed `{:}` |
| `d : Data = {:}` | `Data` | the same |
| `m : Map<String, Int> = {:}` | `Map<String, Int>` | an empty map, equal to `Map<String, Int>([], [])` |
| `m : SortedMap<String, Int> = {:}`, or `OrderedMap` | that map type | an empty map of that kind |
| `f({:})` where `f` takes a `Map<String, Int>` | `Map<String, Int>` | an empty map |

```hql
counts : Map<String, Int> = {:}
counts == Map<String, Int>([], [])
```

```text
true
```

`{:}` is `Data` unless a map type is declared for it. An empty literal has
no entries to infer a type from, so the declared type decides.

This applies to the empty literal only. A non-empty literal with keys is
always `Data`, and binding it to a map type is a type error:

```hql
m : Map<String, Int> = {a: 1}
m
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `m` is declared Map<String, Int> but its value is Data
 --> <expression>:1:24
  |
1 | m : Map<String, Int> = {a: 1}
  |                        ^^^^^^
```

A non-empty map is built with the constructor, `Map(["a"], [1])`.

